Pricing.

[RFC] Continue running price elasticity experiments. Preliminary results:

* Tallinn:
** +15% price increase leads to -12.2% change in RpR, negatively affecting user engagement (share of users with at least 3 rides: -14.27%), thus not stat significant impact on GMV per user (+2.3%);
** -15% price decrease leads to +9.5% change in RpR, positively affecting user engagement (share of users with at least 10 rides: +49.14%). GMV per user is -4.38% (insignificant);
* Riga:
** +10% price increase leads to -5.5% change in RpR. No stat significant impact on GMV per user, engagement rate so far;
** -10% price decrease leads to +7.6% change in RpR. No stat significant impact on GMV per user, engagement rate so far;
* Vilnius:
** +20% price increase leads to -17.5% change in RpR, negatively affecting user engagement (share of users with at least 5 rides: -25.5%). Not stat significant impact on GMV per user: -3.0%;
** -20% price decrease leads to +20.0% change in RpR, positively affecting user engagement (share of users with at least 3 rides: +16.5%). Not stat significant impact on GMV per user: +5.1%;
* Berlin:
** +15% price increase: no stat significant impact on RpR, GMV per user, user engagement so far;
** -15% price increase leads to +13.9% change in RpR, drives user engagement up (share of users with at least 5 rides: +20.1%). Not stat significant impact no GMV per user so far: -5.4%.

Overall, right now we see interesting results that vary depending on market dynamics, but we will continue to conduct the experiments for at least another 1-2 weeks to obtain more conclusive evidence.

