Hi [~accountid:5cde6b461b6dba0dc52c2103] , see my responses below:

{quote}_Does the content of a report made against a driver fall under rider personal data? Or, can we share this with the accused driver as this concerns them as well?_ {quote}

To my understanding, the report contains information about the trip (and sometimes about the Rider as well), and this information would usually be qualified as personal data. *Why:* personal data is information that relates to / can identify a specific individual. Therefore, I would presume that the reports made by rider would always contain personal data, as we/driver would always be able to trace it back to a specific rider. In other words, if we have trip time, destination/pick up location → we can identify the rider → personal data.

{quote}_Would it be possible for us to rephrase and share the initial allegation so the driver could comment and give his side of the events?_{quote}

Yes, I think it makes sense to share at least some information with the Driver after 24 hours have passed (i.e. when the immediate risk to the Rider is no longer there). 

{quote}_I have a similar question to the above - what is meant by details? Are the details Rider details (including pick-up location and destination address) or report details (what was reported )?_{quote}

As commented above - yes, both Rider details and report details would fall under the definition of personal data.

{quote}_Our current processes do not allow any information sharing in the first 24 hours in such cases (as the accused can contact the accusing party). However, after this time has passed, would we be able to let the driver know at least the type of report we received about them?_{quote}

(Same as above) - Yes, I think it makes sense to share at least some information with the Driver after 24 hours have passed (i.e. when the immediate risk to the Rider is no longer there).

