As discussed via Slack, I have raised this issue with external counsel. Please see below the question I addressed to him (in green) and the lawyer's response (in blue), as well as the email chain (attached) for a better understanding. In short, the external counsel considers that selling branded helmets instead of giving them to drivers for free may reduce the risk of reclassification, but would not exclude it entirely.

{color:#36b37e}*BL:* {color}{color:#36b37e}_Our business colleagues wish to provide helmets with the Bolt logo to drivers who will provide parcel delivery services through the Bolt Send category. We are aware that providing branded items and/or work equipment to drivers may increase the risk of reclassification and is one of the criteria that a judge would take into account when determining the existence of an employment relationship between Bolt and the drivers._{color}

{color:#36b37e}_Despite the risk involved in this initiative, our business colleagues are keen to move forward as it would significantly increase Bolt's visibility. For this reason, they have asked us to advise them on measures that contribute to reducing the risk of reclassification that this project may entail. I thought that perhaps if they sold the helmets instead of giving them away, that would reduce or eliminate the risk of reclassification. However, the business told me that they could only sell the helmets for a symbolic price, otherwise the drivers would not acquire them._{color}

{color:#36b37e}_Considering the above circumstances, could you please:_{color}

* {color:#36b37e}_confirm whether selling the branded helmets to the drivers for a symbolic price would be a sufficient measure to mitigate the risk of reclassification? If not,_ {color}
* {color:#36b37e}_propose alternative mitigation measures?_{color}



{color:#4c9aff}*BM:*{color}{color:#4c9aff} _The fact that drivers use branded elements could eventually be considered as work clothes or part of them, which could mean a further presumption of labor relationship in case of claims._{color}

{color:#4c9aff}_Ideally, the helmets should not have distinctive emblems, but we understand that the Company is willing to assume that risk and maintain the logo. Hence, we consider that selling the helmets with the company's logo could be considered as a merchandising issue, in the understanding that the service providers are the ones who voluntarily and by their own means -in this case- acquire the helmets with the logo at a determined price._{color}

{color:#4c9aff}_However this does not imply zero labor contingency, even more when the motorcycle transportation segment has a high risk of accidents, and the lack of social security coverage. That situation could make people more likely to file lawsuits._{color}



